Circular purchase behavior recognizes that consumer choices fundamentally shape product lifecycles and material flows. By designing products and purchase experiences that encourage reuse, sharing, repair, and responsible disposal, companies can shift consumer behavior toward circular participation. This strategy involves making responsible choices easier, more attractive, and more socially rewarding than linear alternatives. Understanding and removing friction from circular purchase decisions—whether buying refurbished products, choosing repairable options, or participating in take-back programs—directly influences whether circular systems gain market share.